#{[17592186541154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541154 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/751d38a1a37afd277cbd54850abf61db898a231b49ad0d4bcaa5964bbab601e1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{4}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb5c025a-0d14-591c-9102-8c187483f1d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541154 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/05/0534847b2363ff75e053b9d05736ff367f2ad6da527b7100de0018d003c4c9df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
